i think 10 or 20 years ago  women had to make a conscious choice  between being a wife  mother a homemaker or having a  professional career  but we aren't willing to be forced into  that choice  and i think our classmates respect us  for it  [Music]  hi susie how are you i'm great hi  barbara hi anne this is so exciting  to be doing this interview together and  be reunited  wonderful what a treat  barbara ann and i had the good fortune  even though we were only  three of five women in our class of mbas  which  numbered about 308  the visionary dean of the stanford  business school gary williams who  admitted us  he put three of us in the same section  and that gave barbara ann and i a chance  to  become close friends and so  at the end of the first year based on  our closeness  we volunteered to do a class project on  marketing  deciding we needed to educate the school  the faculty  the administrators and our fellow  students about what our experience was  like because it was a very compelling  unique experience to be that small a  minority  in a environment that was dominated by  men  we started interviewing one another  taking pictures the full title  is what's a nice girl like you  doing in a place like this that's  exactly the question  that faculty administrators and fellow  students pose to us  for myself it's not that i find  homemaking an inferior role for women  it's simply that i believe that women  should have the freedom to choose  other roles if that's what they'd like  to do if that's where their  talents and interests lie there were  indeed males  in our class who just oozed confidence  and it's expressed in that question  what's a nice girl like you doing in a  place like this  they clearly felt they had every right  in the world  and we solely because we were women  did not i don't mean to be glib  but to me it's when did i take them  seriously  i don't think it really got to me what  were the first couple of months like in  business school  um they were a shock i'm about 10 years  older than either susie or anne  and i had worked for a number of years  in  the real world i was surprised  first of all to be in a room that was  nearly all men  and deeply grateful to see susie and  anne  the camaraderie with barbara and anne  was immediately  helpful i walked into one  class i think it was econ the very first  week of school first year  and the professor turned to me in front  of the  all the students who had already arrived  and he said  you're an mba and from the back row  came this voice she sure  is and that was my future friend  ann thornton i love that susie that's  great  i remember when we got back our first  accounting exams  and the professor put the curve up on  the board  someone from the back of the room  shouted out well how did women's lib do  and suddenly everyone in the room knew  our scores  we really weren't allowed to be  anonymous  people were very interested in our  performance  and perhaps they felt a little  threatened by us  i just had to adapt at the business  school to this  reality that many did not have the same  positive expectations for my success in  the world of  work and in leadership but my family  background my undergraduate  peers ann and barbara  that helped me we were the unusual ones  we were in the unexpected place doing  the unexpected thing  luckily the young culture does not  engage in the kind of discrimination  that the older culture is doing so  i think that it's going to be better it  just seemed that people were saying  things don't have to be the way they've  always been and i think that  the politics of that time i think that's  what  inspired all of us i mean you know this  was  60s 70s and we we thought we could  change the world and we kind of did in  some ways  the curriculum at the stanford business  school at the  time it was actually still an  informative stage and there were  very few women who were  presented as leaders in the case studies  certainly our case studies here at the  business school  have shown us only women who are doing  mindless assembly  line work and who are unable to relate  to other women  except in the most superficial sorts of  ways  and i think that if we can present  ourselves as real people in this program  then maybe they'll understand this  better  [Music]  i felt people began taking me seriously  after we did the marketing presentation  i really felt that we found many  fellow students who became truly great  friends  who saw us as individuals sauce as we  saw ourselves  and that made a big difference  [Music]  and i hope you realize how much your  support  meant to me when we were new students at  the business school and part of  this very very tiny minority you were  able and  to deal so well with these questions  about why we were there  and i took so much encouragement from  that and  barbara it was great for ann and i  to be a close friend to and fellow  student  with a woman who'd made her way in the  business world and  had a lot of wisdom to share with us you  know barbara's no one's fool  and so she always said it like it was  and she always proved herself thank you  if i had a chance to talk to the woman  i was when we made that program i would  tell her to keep doing what she was  doing  she was on the right track she'd have a  wonderful career  she'd always be very grateful for her  stanford graduate school of business  education it was going to  all pay off i would do it again and i  would say  go for it you have nothing to lose now  if i could go back  and talk to myself knowing now what  i've seen in the last 50 years i would  say  hey lady stand up it's your  responsibility  you're good at it go ahead  you
 

